  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 132 AM EDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nCurrently, a strong upper ridge of high pressure is centered to our \nsouthwest over the southern Mississippi River Valley with surface \nhigh pressure over New England. This will continue to promote \nsubsidence with cool morning lows and mild daytime highs. Efficient \nmixing will allow RH's to drop into the 30s and upper 20s with weak \nflow and MSLP gradient keeping winds light. Early in the week, the \nridge will progress eastward with surface high pressure shifting \nalmost directly over the area. The height rises will lead to a \nslight warming trend and continued daytime mixing keeping RH values \nlow. \n\nThis same general pattern will continue through the end of the week \nand into the weekend for Halloween with additional height rises \npotentially pushing highs closer to 80 degrees for some. Otherwise, \nlow RHs and light winds will keep limited fire weather concerns in \nthe forecast each day, but thankfully only about 10 percent of the \narea is in drought at this time.",
